# Intro
Two identical stars (29.7 Msun) 3 pc apart on the x axis, at the centre of a
periodic box of uniform gas (1e3 atom/cm^3, 0.1 Msun particles, 262144
particles, 51.8 smoothing lengths on a side). The gas has Z = 0.01 Zsun, an
absorption length of 32.6 pc in the PE band, so it is nearly transparent
over the box.

The example shows the light of two sources that are close to each other. In
the gas near both stars the light of the two stars is held in the same
reception slot where their directions fall in one slot, and in different
slots where they do not.

`params.yml` fixes the time step at 9.375e-6 (internal units). Cooling is
off (`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`): the gas stays at
rest and the maps show the light only. `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed:
cap` limits the speed of the light to 0.99 c, a limit that the fixed step
never reaches. A packet makes one hop of about one smoothing length per step,
so the light moves at about one smoothing length per step, much slower than
c, and the run lasts a few tens of steps.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. The number of reception
slots is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table that carries MeanPhotonEnergyPE (see the
ISRF documentation). `run.sh` takes `PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5`
from this directory, from `$GEAR_RADIATION_TABLE` or from `~/programs/pychem`,
in that order. `radiation_table=<file>` selects another table.

# Run
```
./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an environment
variable: `n_threads`, `gas_density` (atom/cm^3), `gas_mass` (Msun),
`star_mass` (Msun), `distance_pc`, `n_steps` (last step of the run), `swift_exe`, `run_dir`, `run_analysis`
(0 skips the plot) and `extra_args` (further swift arguments).

# Plot
`run.sh` calls `plotTwoStars.py` from the run directory. It writes
`two_stars.png` from the last snapshot: the kernel-mean PE field in the
plane of the stars, and its values along the line through both stars.
